            view: 'The profitability pivot is working, with gross margins at 60.9% on a better ex-US mix and net debt down to 1.52x. **The genuinely interesting asset is Chestnut Ridge: an "In US for US" plant already supplying about a third of US sales, which is a direct hedge against the 2028 generic tariff schedule at a time when nobody is paying for it.** On its own filed ratios the return has recovered to 15% and then 18% over the last two years.',
            watch: '**The decade is brutal and it is the reason for caution: 57%, 8%, 11%, 16%, 2%, 5%, 8%, minus 5%, then 15% and 18%.** That is a company that has destroyed capital as often as it has earned on it, and the recovery is two years old. The June DEA cycle refused an expanded controlled-substance allocation, stalling the main growth lever — which revealed that the differentiation was rented from a regulator rather than owned. **Note also that this dashboard shows trailing sales of ₹19,114cr and a 9.7% return; the filed accounts show roughly ₹4,859cr and 18%. I have written this off the filed figures.** Buy the plant and the turnaround, not the pipeline.',
