            what: 'An Indian formulator with a large, commoditised US generics book, new oncology and injectable plants, and its first US branded product.',
            view: 'The strategy is correct and clearly articulated: Alembic has concluded that being a price-taker to consolidated US buyers is not a business, and is buying its way out on three fronts. **Complex injectables and oncology carry a genuine capital and regulatory barrier that keeps the crowd out. Ceria, its first US branded product, is the more interesting move** — branded economics are a different game, and if it lands it changes what multiple this company deserves rather than just what it earns. Non-US international markets are doing the near-term work while margins hold at 24.6%, and at 22.3x it is the cheapest branded-adjacent name here.',
